WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.799
During World War II, the Nazis were terrified
of a mysterious female spy that they called

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:06.100
“The Limping Lady.”

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:10.370
Hundreds of posters with her face were plastered
in every major French city, and yet she managed

00:00:10.370 --> 00:00:12.420
to slip by undetected.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.950
This was Virginia Hall: An American spy living
in Nazi-occupied France.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:20.370
She walked with a limp on her prosthetic leg
called “Cuthbert”.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:26.320
Even with her disability, she still managed
to orchestra jailbreaks for POW’s, run resistance

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.440
newspapers, and get vital information back
to the Allies.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.940
Even Klaus Barbie, The Butcher of Lyon, considered
her to be one of the most dangerous Allied

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:37.579
spies, and he was on a personal mission to
hunt her down.

00:00:37.579 --> 00:00:43.269
So, who was Virginia Hall, and how did she
become one of the most successful spies in

00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:45.940
history?

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:59.370
Early Life in Baltimore
Virginia Hall was born on April 6, 1906 in

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:00.760
Baltimore, Maryland.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:05.000
Her father, Edwin Lee Hall, was an entrepreneur
who helped to run banks and movie theaters

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.000
in the city.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.510
Her mother, Barbara, was hired to be his secretary,
and they fell in love.

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:13.220
Virginia had one brother named John, and their
family lived in a mansion in Baltimore.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.890
They would spend summer vacations traveling
through Europe, and she grew up with all of

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.530
the finest things in life.

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:22.290
Virginia’s mother, Barbara was not born
into wealth, so she fully embraced this new

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:23.530
upper-class lifestyle.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:27.180
She wanted Virginia to marry a well-to-do
man in high society.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.030
Barbara made sure that her daughter received
the best education at an all-girls academy

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.500
called The Roland Park Country School.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.930
However, Virginia was not the dainty young
woman that her mother had hoped for.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:42.390
She always played the male roles in the school
plays, because she wanted to perform the sword

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:45.350
fights and become the hero who saved the day.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.260
When she was outside of her school uniform,
she dressed in trousers and checkered shirts.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:53.390
After school, she would go through the woods
to hunt rabbits, and even wore live snakes

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:54.600
as bracelets.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.510
Her classmates respected her confidence and
self-assurance, so they voted her in as the

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:02.840
editor of the school newspaper, and class
president.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.580
Virginia was studying languages at her prep
school, and she told everyone that she dreamed

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:11.690
of becoming a diplomat so that she could travel
around the world and have amazing adventures.

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:15.370
Her father fully embraced her ambition, and
helped pay for language tutoring.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:20.390
However, her mother continued to push her
to act like a dignified young woman in Baltimore

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:21.400
society.

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.440
After graduating from High School, Virginia
studied at some of the most prestigious women’s

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.760
universities in the country.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:31.700
She attended both Barnard College in New York
City, and Radcliffe, which is apart of Harvard

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:32.700
University.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.060
Her mother had been scheming for years to
set Virgina up with men who she considered

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.370
to be “good enough” to marry her daughter.

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:42.549
She arranged for Virginia to court a young
man when she was just 19 years old.

00:02:42.549 --> 00:02:47.560
They became engaged rather quickly, because
their families wanted to unite their businesses.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.209
Unfortunately, her mother Barbara didn’t
seem to realize that just because a man has

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:55.819
a trust fund and an Ivy League education,
that doesn’t make him a good person.

00:02:55.819 --> 00:03:00.170
Virginia's fiance was cheating on her constantly,
and they were not actually in love with one

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:01.170
another.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:05.780
She knew that if she chose this path that
her parents wanted for her, she would never

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:06.780
be happy.

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:12.739
In the end, she chose to follow her dream
of becoming that hero who went on great adventures.

00:03:12.739 --> 00:03:19.569
Life in Europe
Virginia listened to her intuition, and broke

00:03:19.569 --> 00:03:22.510
off her engagement so that she could move
to France.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:27.269
She transferred to a University in Paris called
Ecole Libre Des Sciences Politiques.

00:03:27.269 --> 00:03:31.439
During her time living in Europe as a student,
she was able to practice the language with

00:03:31.439 --> 00:03:36.330
other native speakers, and became fluent in
French, Spanish, Russian, German, and Italian.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:40.980
However, when she spoke, it was always a dead-giveaway
that she was a foreigner, because she could

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.930
never shake her American accent.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:46.760
After spending the end of her college years
enjoying the Paris nightlife in the roaring

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:49.180
20’s, she moved to Vienna, Austria.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.560
This is where she fell in love with a Polish
army officer named Emil.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.540
They wanted to get married, but her trip to
Europe was fully funded by her father.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:00.739
Out of respect for her parents, Virginia wrote
to her father to ask his permission.

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:05.489
Sadly, he forbade her from marrying Emil,
because he believed that her life in Europe

00:04:05.489 --> 00:04:06.689
was just a phase.

00:04:06.689 --> 00:04:10.299
Her parents still wanted her to move back
to the United States to carry on the family

00:04:10.299 --> 00:04:12.059
legacy in Baltimore.

00:04:12.059 --> 00:04:15.529
She obeyed her father's wishes, and broke
things off with Emil.

00:04:15.529 --> 00:04:19.680
Virginia returned to the United States 1929
just in time to witness the beginning of the

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.680
Great Depression.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.800
Her family’s finances were hit so hard,
they lost almost everything.

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.460
To make matters worse, her father died of
a sudden heart attack from all of the stress

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:31.110
of his life crumbling around him.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:34.490
This left their family even more financially
destitute than before.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:39.139
Now, that snobbery and sense of entitlement
that her mother held onto for so long was

00:04:39.139 --> 00:04:40.139
gone.

00:04:40.139 --> 00:04:43.949
The family was no longer apart of high society,
and Virginia was free to pursue her dreams

00:04:43.949 --> 00:04:45.060
once again.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.271
She decided that she would leave the United
States and apply for job at the American Embassy

00:04:49.271 --> 00:04:55.180
in Warsaw, Poland as the Consular Service
Clerk making $2,000 a year, which is roughly

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:56.770
$30,000 after inflation.

00:04:56.770 --> 00:04:59.950
It’s probably not a coincidence that she
chose to move to Poland.

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:04.330
It had not been very long since she broke
off her engagement with Emil, and she probably

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:06.820
hoped that she could find him again.

00:05:06.820 --> 00:05:08.310
Sadly, that never happened.

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:12.080
Most historians believe that he would have
died during World War II, since he had a career

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.090
in the Polish military, and the Nazis killed
so many people when they occupied Poland.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:20.139
While working at the embassy in Warsaw, Virginia
was given administrative tasks like decoding

00:05:20.139 --> 00:05:22.180
messages and filing paperwork.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:25.370
This was nothing like the adventurous life
she imagined.

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:28.389
She took a diplomat’s entrance exam, and
scored 100%.

00:05:28.389 --> 00:05:32.759
With her language skills, Ivy-League education,
and stellar resume, she should have been able

00:05:32.759 --> 00:05:35.190
to easily move out of a clerical position.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:39.940
Every time there was a job opening, she tried
to apply, and they would “lose” her application.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:42.069
Virginia could tell that the odds were stacked
against her.

00:05:42.069 --> 00:05:46.450
She believed that maybe the problem was isolated
to Poland, so she decided to transfer to the

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:48.770
American embassy in Izmir, Turkey.

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:52.800
Once she was living in Turkey, she tried to
make friends with her new co-workers.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:56.740
Despite having so many setbacks, she still
hoped that maybe she could become one of the

00:05:56.740 --> 00:05:59.889
special few women who were accepted as diplomats.

00:05:59.889 --> 00:06:04.990
But the plans for her future career were completely
ruined when she was on a hunting trip with

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:06.340
some friends.

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:10.250
Virginia was trying to climb over a wire fence
when her gun accidentally went off, and she

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:12.419
shot herself point-blank in the foot.

00:06:12.419 --> 00:06:14.120
She fell, and sank into the mud.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.240
Dirt and bacteria poured into the hole in
her shattered foot.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.169
Her friends wrapped her foot in a tunicate,
and carried her to the car.

00:06:20.169 --> 00:06:24.180
She was in the hospital for three weeks, but
bacteria had gotten into her open wound, and

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:27.759
it caused an infection that began to spread
throughout her entire leg.

00:06:27.759 --> 00:06:31.830
Just a few days before Christmas, he leg had
to be amputated from the knee down.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:35.820
At 27 years old, she became permanently disabled
for the rest of her life.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:39.020
The doctors gave her a wooden peg leg, and
she named it “Cuthbert.”

00:06:39.020 --> 00:06:42.840
After learning to walk with her prosthetic,
Virginia was still determined to continue

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:44.710
working towards her goal.

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:49.050
She applied to work at the consulate in Venice,
Italy, and passed the oral language assessment

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.050
with flying colors.

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:54.090
But once they saw her leg, they rejected her
immediately, and forbid her from taking the

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:55.750
diplomat’s exam.

00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:59.400
Virginia was fuming, so she wrote a letter
to the Secretary of State, Cordell Hull.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:04.180
He wrote back that “Hall could become a
fine career girl at the Consular Service”

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:08.210
as in- he thought she should continue to be
a clerical assistant, along with the rest

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:10.169
of the women who worked at the embassies.

00:07:10.169 --> 00:07:13.970
The Secretary of State officially banned her
from ever applying for higher positions again.

00:07:13.970 --> 00:07:17.620
She had absolutely no hope left of ever becoming
a diplomat.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:25.319
Life as an SOE Spy
For the next few years, Virginia Hall kept

00:07:25.319 --> 00:07:29.440
her head down, and continued to do clerical
work, but she was clearly unhappy.

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:34.449
Even after her aspirations had been shot down,
she still dreamed of going on those adventures.

00:07:34.449 --> 00:07:37.740
After World War II began, Virginia felt compelled
to help in any way she could.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.610
So she decided to leave her job and move back
to France in 1939.

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:46.759
She was now 33 years old, and completely started
her life over from scratch as an ambulance

00:07:46.759 --> 00:07:47.759
driver.

00:07:47.759 --> 00:07:51.910
Just one year later, in 1940, Nazi Germany
occupied France, and Virginia had to escape

00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:56.120
through the Pyrenees mountains in Spain and
France on a trail known as “Le Chemin de

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:57.819
la Liberté”, or “The Freedom Trail”.

00:07:57.819 --> 00:08:02.719
The journey is 72 km or 44-miles, with elevations
as high as 6,000 feet.

00:08:02.719 --> 00:08:06.539
This typically takes 3 to 4 days for even
able-bodied people to complete, and it would

00:08:06.539 --> 00:08:09.860
have taken Virgina Hall even longer with her
disability.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:14.270
During the course of the war, over 2,000 experienced
mountain guides who lead groups through The

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:18.699
Freedom Trail were captured and executed by
the Nazis, and thousands of civilians were

00:08:18.699 --> 00:08:19.699
taken prisoner.

00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:23.889
Somehow, despite the pain she experienced
with her prosthetic leg, Virginia Hall climbed

00:08:23.889 --> 00:08:26.439
through the mountain trails undetected.

00:08:26.439 --> 00:08:30.069
Once she arrived in England, Virginia Hall
went straight to the American Embassy in London

00:08:30.069 --> 00:08:35.519
to volunteer her services to the British Special
Operations Executive, or the “SOE”.

00:08:35.519 --> 00:08:39.430
Now that the nation was at war, the English
knew that they could use her special abilities.

00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:42.691
They were impressed with her language skills,
and agreed to bring her on as one of their

00:08:42.691 --> 00:08:43.691
spies.

00:08:43.691 --> 00:08:48.610
The SOE trained her in weaponry, security,
communications, as well as resistance activity.

00:08:48.610 --> 00:08:50.990
They gave her the code name “Heckler”.

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:54.421
Once she received her training, Virginia had
to hike back into France on foot through the

00:08:54.421 --> 00:08:56.160
Pyrenees mountains once again.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:00.120
During the journey, she ran into some issues,
and contacted the SOE, saying “Cuthbert”

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.410
was giving her trouble.

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:04.930
They responded, “If Cuthbert is giving you
difficulty, have him eliminated.”

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:07.500
They had no idea she was talking about her
leg.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:12.221
In 1941, she returned to france with false
identification, and pretended to be a newspaper

00:09:12.221 --> 00:09:14.209
reporter from The New York Post.

00:09:14.209 --> 00:09:18.360
She was actually publishing secret intelligence
that she had gathered from a spy network that

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:19.360
she created.

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:22.970
More than 2 million people in France were
able to get valuable information through these

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:24.260
resistance newspapers.

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:28.170
Whenever the British SOE spies made their
way into France, they had to report to this

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:30.160
mysterious “Agent Heckler.”

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.540
Every single day was a new adventure for her.

00:09:32.540 --> 00:09:36.970
On several occasions, she saved the lives
of Allied pilots who survived from their downed

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:37.970
aircrafts.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:41.640
Any time she recovered a pilot or a prisoner,
she brought them to a safe houses where they

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:43.190
could receive medical attention.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:47.270
As time went on Virginia, began to plan more
and more ideas about how they could help the

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:48.270
Allies.

00:09:48.270 --> 00:09:52.260
Together with a French doctor named Jean Rousset,
they plotted jail breaks to free Prisoners

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:53.740
of War from Nazi camps.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:57.910
Dr. Rousset worked at a mental asylum, and
they had to transport patients on a regular

00:09:57.910 --> 00:09:58.910
basis.

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:02.510
So, as long as the POW’s could pretend to
be mentally ill in the back of the ambulance,

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:05.029
they were able to bring the men across the
checkpoints.

00:10:05.029 --> 00:10:08.290
Then, Virginia would help guide them on a
multi-day journey into the United Kingdom

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:10.380
on The Freedom Trail.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:15.510
Of course, Virginia Hall was far from being
the only Allied spy who was working in France.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:17.959
She was just one of the most successful.

00:10:17.959 --> 00:10:22.670
During World War II, Great Britain sent 400
SOE agents into France, and 100 of them were

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:23.690
captured and killed.

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:26.790
To the Nazis, she was just a helpless woman
who walked with a limp.

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:30.829
However, while her physical disability should
have made her less suspicious, it also was

00:10:30.829 --> 00:10:35.589
a very specific train that made her stand
out from the crowd.

00:10:35.589 --> 00:10:38.560
It was rare to see a young woman walking with
a limp and a prosthetic leg.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:41.680
The more Virginia went above and beyond to
save people, the more she earned a reputation

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:42.680
for herself.

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:46.449
News began spread that a limping woman named
Agent Heckler was helping the Allied troops

00:10:46.449 --> 00:10:47.600
in plain site.

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:51.510
Klaus Barbie was the Chief of the Gestapo
in Lyon, France, and he heard the news of

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:54.570
Agent Heckler and her elaborate POW jail breaks.

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:57.899
No one knew who “Heckler” truly was, or
what country she came from.

00:10:57.899 --> 00:11:00.110
The only dead giveaway was her accent.

00:11:00.110 --> 00:11:04.060
However, Klaus Barbie must have had a hard
time believing an American could be so fluent

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:08.699
in French, so he was once quoted saying, “I
would give anything to get my hands on that

00:11:08.699 --> 00:11:10.390
limping Canadian bitch.”

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:16.089
Barbie ordered his men to hang over 200 posters
of Virginia Hall’s face, and it read: “The

00:11:16.089 --> 00:11:17.089
Limping Lady.

00:11:17.089 --> 00:11:18.350
The most dangerous of all Allied spies.

00:11:18.350 --> 00:11:20.421
We must find and destroy her.”

00:11:20.421 --> 00:11:24.660
They put a bounty on her head, offering a
reward for anyone who could come forward with

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:26.490
any information about her whereabouts.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:31.340
Eventually, someone was tempted enough to
turn her secret location in to the Gestapo.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:35.530
In November of 1942, she sent a message back
to London that the address of her safe house

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:36.940
had been compromised.

00:11:36.940 --> 00:11:40.589
They ordered her to run, but she told them
that before she could escape, she must save

00:11:40.589 --> 00:11:44.150
two captured SEO agents named Alex and Fabian.

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:49.050
However, just as she was attempting to break
them out of jail, a huge influx of Nazi soldiers

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.050
showed up.

00:11:50.050 --> 00:11:52.640
They had just arrived after defeating territories
in North Africa.

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:56.500
These were thousands of fresh eyes who were
seeing the wanted posters for the very first

00:11:56.500 --> 00:12:00.640
time, and they would be on the lookout for
the young woman with the distinctive limp.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:04.649
Once she saw the new soldiers, it was clear
to Virginia that if she did not leave immediately,

00:12:04.649 --> 00:12:05.649
she would be captured.

00:12:05.649 --> 00:12:09.240
She packed her bags and left town just in
the nick of time, because the very next day,

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:14.579
Dr. Jean Rousset was arrested and killed for
running his underground escape route in the

00:12:14.579 --> 00:12:15.579
asylum.

00:12:15.579 --> 00:12:17.690
The Gestapo burst in the Heckler HQ, but she
was already gone.

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:23.290
Virginia Hall just barely had a 24-hour head
start making her way back on the Freedom Trail.

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:26.180
It was just enough time to save her life.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:30.750
However, just as Virginia Hall could practically
see her freedom on the horizon, the Spanish

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:33.480
police arrested her for illegally crossing
the border.

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:34.480
She was brought to jail.

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:40.730
At the very least- she wasn’t in the hands
of the Nazis, and could finally get some rest.

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:47.860
Joining the CIA
We are not sure just how long Virginia Hall

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:52.320
spent living in a Spanish jail cell, but she
had to contact the American embassy in order

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:53.690
for them to finally release her.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:57.089
Once she was free, Virginia contacted the
SOE, and let them know that she was alive

00:12:57.089 --> 00:12:58.089
and well.

00:12:58.089 --> 00:13:01.160
After spending time in prison, she was even
more eager to get back to helping with the

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:02.160
war effort.

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:06.230
She requested to be sent back into France,
because she felt that her duty was not over.

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:10.750
They refused, saying that the Gestapo already
knew who she was, and they were right.

00:13:10.750 --> 00:13:14.600
Illustrations of Virginia's likeness were
hung on “Wanted” posters all over Nazi-occupied

00:13:14.600 --> 00:13:15.600
Europe.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:16.600
It would be far too risky.

00:13:16.600 --> 00:13:22.079
As far as the SOE was concerned, Agent Heckler
had done her duty, and they didn’t expect

00:13:22.079 --> 00:13:23.190
anything more from her.

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:26.720
Once she knew that the British would not take
her back, she took a break from being a spy,

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:27.720
until 1944.

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:30.720
This is when she learned about the planned
Allied invasion of Normandy.

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:34.829
She met up with the American Office of Strategic
Services, or the “OSS” for short.

00:13:34.829 --> 00:13:36.730
This was the precursor of the CIA.

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:40.459
Since her face was recognizable, they helped
her to dress up as an elderly woman.

00:13:40.459 --> 00:13:44.819
This was actually the perfect disguise, because
it looked totally natural for an older woman

00:13:44.819 --> 00:13:45.819
to be limping.

00:13:45.819 --> 00:13:50.339
They also taught her morse code, and she learned
how to become a radio operator to send messages

00:13:50.339 --> 00:13:52.649
back to the Americans as quickly as possible.

00:13:52.649 --> 00:13:56.610
After returning to France as an old woman,
Virginia made goat’s milk and lived out

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.630
a daily existence as a milkmaid.

00:13:58.630 --> 00:14:02.670
Yet again, the Nazis thought she was harmless,
so Virginia was able to hide in plain site

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:07.579
delivering milk and cheese to the camps as
the Germans openly discussed top-secret information

00:14:07.579 --> 00:14:08.579
in front of her.

00:14:08.579 --> 00:14:11.480
They had no idea that she could speak fluent
German, of course.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:15.570
After identifying their most vital locations,
Virginia contacted the Allies over the radio,

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:19.910
and helped to orchestra the bombings of several
bridges and major Nazi infrastructures throughout

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:23.899
France before the Allies ever reached the
shores of Normandy on D-Day.

00:14:23.899 --> 00:14:28.399
Even in the midst of battle, she was on the
ground sabotaging as much Nazi gear as she

00:14:28.399 --> 00:14:29.450
possibly could.

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:33.529
The Americans celebrated their victory over
the Nazis, and no one ever knew that they

00:14:33.529 --> 00:14:37.920
needed to thank Virginia Hall for prepping
the road to victory beforehand.

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:45.610
After the War, and Legacy
When the war was finally over in 1945, Virginia

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:47.500
Hall was recognized for serving her country.

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:51.450
She was the one and only woman to receive
The Distinguished Service Cross of the United

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:52.699
States of America.

00:14:52.699 --> 00:14:55.820
Even though this was such a prestigious honor,
and the world would have been shocked to know

00:14:55.820 --> 00:15:00.190
just how much she contributed to the Allied
victory, she requested for the award ceremony

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:01.330
to be held in secret.

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:05.000
She did not want the newspapers to show pictures
of her face, because she wanted to continue

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:06.410
working as a spy.

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:11.380
The Office of Strategic Services was renamed
The Central Intelligence Agency, or the CIA,

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:15.440
and Virgina Hall would go on to become one
of their very first female secret agents.

00:15:15.440 --> 00:15:18.860
After years of focusing on her career, she
finally got married when she was 44 years

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:21.699
old to a fellow spy named Paul Goillot.

00:15:21.699 --> 00:15:25.130
The CIA gave Virginia and Paul husband new
aliases, and they were sent her to work in

00:15:25.130 --> 00:15:27.949
Venice, Italy to monitor the progress of Communism
in the country.

00:15:27.949 --> 00:15:31.860
For several years, she gathered data on financial
and political information and transmitted

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:33.209
it back to the United States.

00:15:33.209 --> 00:15:37.079
Then, she joined a division of the CIA called
National Committee For Free Europe before

00:15:37.079 --> 00:15:39.089
retiring when she turned 60 years old.

00:15:39.089 --> 00:15:43.009
Even after she was retired, she knew that
the missions she participated in were still

00:15:43.009 --> 00:15:44.009
top-secret.

00:15:44.009 --> 00:15:47.940
So she never revealed the details of her exploits,
and she never gave a single interview.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:51.750
She lived to be 76 years old, and died in
1982.

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:56.769
Years after her death, a CIA agent named Craig
R. Gralley read the documents of Virginia

00:15:56.769 --> 00:16:01.509
Hall’s incredible story, and he felt inspired
to retrace her steps on The Freedom Trail.

00:16:01.509 --> 00:16:05.290
He wanted to experience what it must have
been like for her to go through the mountains.

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:10.120
Even as an able-bodied man, he stumbled multiple
times, and almost fell to his death.

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:14.360
Gralley wrote, “I’m hiking above 6,000
feet and have stumbled twice on this narrow

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:16.850
and rocky trail deep in the French Pyrenees.

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:21.500
There’s nothing to grab on to if I fall;
only thorny brush and an occasional scrub

00:16:21.500 --> 00:16:23.920
pine cling to the steep mountain slope.”

00:16:23.920 --> 00:16:26.660
Today, she continues to inspire people with
her story.

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:30.570
As a spy, Virginia Hall kept most of her life
a secret, because she was fighting to keep

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:32.269
people safe from the Nazi regime.

00:16:32.269 --> 00:16:36.759
She worked quietly behind the scenes without
ever expecting fame or glory.

00:16:36.759 --> 00:16:57.600
In the end, she truly did accomplish her dream
of going down in history as a true hero.

